data.value:
**Apply renaming** renames the files on disk, once, with the final names of the chain. A question in the top bar asks you to confirm first.

**Conflicts.** Every step shows in red the names that cannot be used, so that they are fixed before applying:
• two files get the same name;
• a forbidden character: `< > : " / \ | ? *`;
• a name reserved by Windows: `CON`, `PRN`, `AUX`, `NUL`, `COM1` to `COM9`, `LPT1` to `LPT9`;
• a name ending with a dot or a space, or an empty name;
• a path longer than 259 characters.
Just before renaming, the disk is checked again: a file may have appeared in the folder since it was loaded.

**Safe renaming.** Each file first takes a temporary name, then its final name. That is how swaps (A becomes B and B becomes A), cycles and case-only changes (`photo.jpg` → `Photo.jpg`, which Windows would not see otherwise) work. If anything fails half way, everything done so far is undone.

**Undo.** After a renaming, **Undo this renaming** gives every file its previous name back. Each renaming is written in a journal as it goes, in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\ChainRenamer\journals`.

The pattern. The essentials:
• `\d` a digit, `\s` a space, `.` any character, `\.` a real dot;
• `+` once or more, `*` any number of times, `?` optional;
• `( )` a group, put back by `$1`, `$2`…;
• `^` the start of the name, `$` its end;
• `[abc]` one of these characters, `a|b` a or b.
